      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "Models ship in three or four fixed sizes. Traffic does not: it swings all day. So you either pay for the big model at 4 a.m. or serve the small one at noon, and making a size in between means producing, storing and swapping in another model.\nElastify makes one model every size. Take a sentence-embedding model, remove 60% of its FLOPs at runtime, and its search quality holds at 77% of the original. Cut it the obvious way and it holds at 46%. The cut version runs 1.69× faster on a laptop GPU. There is no retraining and no new weights — the only thing it adds is a 38 KB file."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "The idea is from Your Transformer Is Secretly Elastic (Simoncini, Varghese, Burghouts and Snoek, October 2026). Score every MLP neuron and attention head in a pretrained transformer, refine those scores by gradient descent across many budgets at once with the weights frozen, and the ranking that comes out prunes the model to any size, smoothly. The paper tests 27 vision and text encoders from 22 million to 8 billion parameters. It prunes DINOv3 ViT-H+/16 to 50% sparsity, removing 427 million parameters for a 0.8% drop in ImageNet linear-probe accuracy, and a ViT-B/16 at 60% sparsity runs up to 1.80× faster on an A100.\nElastify is my implementation for Hugging Face models, under 450 lines, built and published a week after the paper appeared. One call learns the ranking. After that, you either slice out a real, smaller model or run the dense one at any budget in place, and switch between budgets in milliseconds."
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "value": "```python\nelastic = elastify.fit(model, batches)   # ~1,000 unlabelled texts; a few minutes\nelastic.save(\"minilm.elastic.pt\")        # 38 KB: the whole ranking\n\nsmall = elastic.prune(0.4)               # a real nn.Module with 40% fewer FLOPs\nwith elastic.sparsity(0.6):              # or the dense model at a budget, in place\n    emb = model(**inputs).last_hidden_state\n```"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "value": "<figure class=\"fig\">\n<span class=\"fig-title\">Figure 2 &#183; How the ranking is learned</span>\n<div class=\"fig-steps fig-steps--v\">\n<div class=\"fig-step\"><b>Score</b><em>Estimate how much every MLP neuron and attention head matters, from squared gradients on unlabelled data.</em></div>\n<i class=\"fig-arr\">&#8595;</i>\n<div class=\"fig-step\"><b>Put on one scale</b><em>Centre neuron and head scores on their medians so both kinds of unit share a single ranking.</em></div>\n<i class=\"fig-arr\">&#8595;</i>\n<div class=\"fig-step fig-step--hi\"><b>Train 14 budgets at once</b><em>Soft-prune at every budget from 5% to 70% together and move only the scores. The weights never change.</em></div>\n<i class=\"fig-arr\">&#8595;</i>\n<div class=\"fig-step\"><b>Cut</b><em>Walk the ranking from the top and keep units until the budget is spent. Any budget, in milliseconds.</em></div>\n</div>\n<figcaption>Each budget's cutoff sends gradient to the units near it, so fourteen budgets together shape the whole ranking instead of one point on it.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "value": "## It is faster, not just smaller\n\n| FLOPs removed | throughput vs. dense | with GPU-aligned widths |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 20% | 1.07&#215; | 1.17&#215; |\n| 40% | 1.25&#215; | 1.37&#215; |\n| 60% | 1.69&#215; | 1.82&#215; |\n\nfp16, batch 128 &#215; 256 tokens, eager PyTorch, a laptop RTX 5060. Aligned widths round each MLP to a multiple of 64 neurons."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "It needs no labels. A classifier is scored against its own predictions, and an encoder against its own ability to tell the samples in a batch apart. Blocks are found by the names of their linear layers, so the same code covers BERT-style encoders (BERT, RoBERTa, MiniLM, BGE, E5, GTE), vision transformers (ViT, DeiT, DINOv2, CLIP and SigLIP towers) and decoders like Qwen3, Llama and Mistral."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "The fine print. Every number above is one small model, all-MiniLM-L6-v2, on two benchmarks — the paper is the source for the big ones. Its parameter count falls only from 22.7M to 16.0M at the 60% budget, because the 11.7M-parameter embedding table costs no FLOPs and is left alone. In decoders with grouped-query attention, a pruned head is zeroed rather than removed: the model behaves exactly as pruned but saves no time on it. Models with a fused QKV projection, like GPT-2 and timm's ViTs, are not supported yet. FLOPs throughout are those of the attention and MLP blocks, the part pruning can touch. Its 23 tests run in seconds."
      }
    ]
  }
